{"number": 50, "text": "                                                                                     Section B Stress in words and phrases\n\n\n                     Exercises\n\n             ‘2.1\n                    Think about the words in bold in these dialogues and underline them if they are likely to be\n                    prominent. Then  listen and check your answers.\n             B27\n                    1  A: Do you  know  of any good  restaurants in Brockhurst?\n                       B: Well, I haven’t been for some years, but there used to be some very good ones.\n                          The Oyster  was the place to eat seafood.\n                       A: Mmm.  I  do like seafood.\n                       B: But I’m sure any of the restaurants there will be good.\n\n                    2  A: Try turning the tap off.\n                       B: I have tried turning it, but it’s stuck.\n                       A: Did you  ask anyone for help?\n                        : No. Look,  why  don’t you try?\n                       B:\n                        : Okay. Hmmm.    There  must  be some\n                       A\n                          way of doing  it.\n                        : I did tell you it was stuck.\n                       >\n                        : There. It just needed some strength!\n                          Anyone  could have  done it.\n\n\n                    Now  listen again. Press ‘pause’ before each B part and read it aloud. Then press ‘play’ again and\n                    compare  your pronunciation with what  follows. Then do the same for the A parts.\n\n             2.2\n                    Play the recording. Press ‘pause’ before each B part and read it aloud. Then press  again and\n                    compare  your pronunciation with what  follows. Did you put prominence in the same places?\n              B28\n\n                    1 A:  There you  are. All finished. B: Is that it?\n                    2  A: You can’t sit there, it’s Kate’s place. B: Oh, is it?\n                    3 A: Are  we there yet? B: Yes, this is it.\n                      A:  Have  you finished? B: Yes, that’s it.\n                    4\n                         Which   coat is yours? B: That’s it.\n                      A:\n                    5\n                          Your train’s arriving. B: This is it, then.\n                    6\n                      A:\n                          Do you  like my painting? B: What  is it?\n                       A:\n                    7\n                    8  A: Come  on, get up now.  B: I can’t. That’s just it.\n                      A:\n\n             2.3\n                    You will hear seven sentences. After each sentence, press ‘pause’ and say one of the responses in the\n                    box. (Make sure you make  the last word prominent.) When you press      again you will hear the\n              B29\n                    correct answer.\n\n                       Who   could it have been?                       Where  was  he from?\n                       There  was  nothing we  could have done.\n                       How   many   is it for?                         But isn’t there anything I can do?\n                       Yes, but I don’t know  who  she was.\n\n\n                    EXAMPLE   You  hear   I thought you were going  to sell your old car.\n                              You  say    1 wish! HAD.\n\n                      Follow up: Try to use the phrase ‘THIS is IT’ (= | agree) in the next conversation you have in English.\n                      Make sure you pronounce it correctly.\n\n\n                                                                        English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3658, "mean_conf": 93.24, "word_count": 439, "words": [{"text": "‘2.1", "x0": 244, "y0": 354, "x1": 318, "y1": 392, "conf": 61, "bold": false}, {"text": "B27", "x0": 255, "y0": 422, "x1": 319, "y1": 453, "conf": 61, "bold": fals